---
// The NatGlass disclosed case study (issue #129). Q2 answered: there are no
// external customers to quote yet, so the trust track leads with the most
// honest story available — the founder's own company running the product in
// production, affiliation stated plainly. Numbers verified against prod
// 2026-08-10; update them when they age.
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { config } from '../../config';

const stats = [
  { n: '3,224', label: 'bid line items priced' },
  { n: '143', label: 'projects run' },
  { n: '1,160', label: 'contacts managed' },
  { n: '17', label: 'months in production' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="How a Glazing Contractor Runs on BuildWorkPro — Customer Story"
  description="National Glass and Construction runs its whole operation — 400+ bids, 143 projects — on BuildWorkPro. Full disclosure: it's the founder's own company. That's the point."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-12">
      <div class="text-center">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3">
          Customer story
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight text-balance">
          The first customer was the founder's own crew
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-slate-500 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          National Glass and Construction — a South Florida glass and glazing contractor — runs its
          entire operation on BuildWorkPro: every bid, every project, every contact.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The disclosure IS the credibility -->
    <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-16">
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-brand-100 bg-brand-50 p-8">
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-3">Full disclosure, up front</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          National Glass and Construction is our founder's own company. BuildWorkPro was built
          inside it — by a contractor who needed the software, not a software company guessing at
          what contractors need. We could hide that and print an anonymous testimonial; we'd rather
          tell you the truth: <strong
            >every feature below ran real jobs at a real contractor before it ever reached you.</strong
          >
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Live production numbers -->
    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4">
        {
          stats.map((s) => (
            <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 text-center">
              <div class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-brand-600 tabular-nums">{s.n}</div>
              <div class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-500">{s.label}</div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-slate-400 text-center">
        Live production numbers as of August 2026 — not projections.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- The story -->
    <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
        <h2>The problem was the quote for the software</h2>
        <p>
          Glazing work is bid-heavy: storefronts, impact windows and doors, shower enclosures,
          railings — each one priced from supplier parts, labor, and margin, sent as a proposal, and
          billed as the work progresses. The software that promised to manage that workflow either
          charged per user, buried pay applications behind enterprise tiers, or was built for the
          general contractor on the other side of the table.
        </p>
        <p>
          So National Glass ran on spreadsheets — until its owner started building the tool he
          couldn't buy.
        </p>

        <h2>What a real contractor runs every week</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Bidding from a catalog:</strong> supplier parts and labor rates priced into line-item
            bids — more than 400 bids and 3,200+ priced line items since going live — with margin applied
            systematically instead of re-derived in a spreadsheet each time.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Projects, not paperwork piles:</strong> 143 projects with their documents, phases,
            and billing in one record — the job's whole history findable in seconds.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A CRM that remembers:</strong> 1,160 contacts — GCs, property managers, suppliers,
            homeowners — with every bid and job tied to them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Books that agree:</strong> invoices and payments synced to QuickBooks Online, so the
            office isn't re-typing what the field already recorded.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Why this matters to you</h2>
        <p>
          Most construction software is designed in an office and tested in a demo. This one gets
          tested on Monday mornings — real bids that have to go out, real pay applications a GC has
          to approve, real crews on real jobs. When something is slow, confusing, or wrong, it costs
          National Glass money before any customer ever sees it, so it gets fixed. You're not buying
          a promise about how contractors work. You're buying the tool one already runs on.
        </p>
        <p>
          Read <a href="/about/">the founder's story</a>, or see the workflow National Glass runs:
          <a href="/features/construction-bidding/">bidding</a>,
          <a href="/features/pay-applications/">pay applications</a>, and
          <a href="/features/project-management/">project management</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 text-center">
      <div class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-900 px-8 py-14 sm:px-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight text-balance">
          Run your operation the same way
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-slate-400 max-w-xl mx-auto">
          Everything National Glass uses — bids, pay applications, change orders, e-signatures,
          QuickBooks sync — is in the one plan. No tiers, no per-user fees.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-center gap-4">
          <a
            href={config.signupUrl}
            class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-8 py-3.5 rounded-xl bg-brand-600 hover:bg-brand-700 text-white font-semibold text-sm transition-all hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:shadow-lg"
          >
            Start free trial
          </a>
          <a
            href="/pricing/"
            class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-8 py-3.5 rounded-xl bg-white/10 text-white font-semibold text-sm hover:bg-white/20 transition-colors"
          >
            See pricing
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-500">
          $79/month flat · 14-day free trial · No credit card required
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
